speechtext:
Hon. J. A. ROBB (Minister of Finance) moved that the House go into committee to consider the following proposed resolution:
Resolved, that it is expedient to bring in the measure to amend The Special War Revenue Act, 1915, as passed by both houses of parliament at the last session but not assented to, providing
(a)	for the removal of the one cent additional postage on letters for transmission by post for any distance within Canada; excepting "drop" letters and post cards;
(b)	for the remission of the stamp tax on receipts;
(c)	for a straight tax on playing cards, on every fifty-four cards or fraction thereof in each package-ten cents per pack;
(d)	for a provision to remove doubts as to whether goods are liable to sales tax when sold under process of law, or under a lien, etc.;
(e)	for the exemption of certain specified articles from consumption or sales tax;
(f)	for adding certain specified articles to the list subject to 50 per cent of the sales tax;
(g)	for removing the tax on carbonic acid
gas;	_
(h)	for a refund of tax paid on ships' stores;
(i)	for the removal of excise taxes on automobiles valued at not more than $1,200, when the conditions prescribed in the said legislation are fulfilled as to importation for any British country under the British preferential tariff, or from countries enjoying most favoured nation treatment, or as to automobiles produced or manufactured in Canada, and authorizing a refund of excise tax on such automobiles new, unused and unsold in the possession of the dealer or agent on 8th June, 1926; and
(j)	for the repeal of certain orders in council relating to the operation of these provisions
He said:	Mr. Speaker, before you leave
the chair I may say that this resolution covers legislation arising from the budget of 1926. The measure to be submitted is identically the same as that which passed both houses of parliament, but which failed to receive royal assent. The principal parts of the legislation were made effective by order in council dated July 14, 1926. There is no change whatever in the general principle of the legislation.
